patches.behavior:
State per loop index: `{ errorMessage: "", pending: null, settled: null }`. `pending` is `{ generation }` while an asynchronous open is awaiting its result. Shared per runtime: a module-level `WeakMap<RuntimeServices, Map<string, number>>` maps `"<componentPath>/<patchId>"` to the last frame a URL was opened.

Each frame:
```
let opened = false, failed = false;
if (state.settled) { state.settled.ok ? (opened = true, state.errorMessage = "") : (failed = true, state.errorMessage = state.settled.message); state.settled = null; }
if (ctx.pulsed("open")) {
  const problem = validate(ctx.input("url"));
  if (problem) { failed = true; state.errorMessage = problem; }
  else if (openedThisFrame(ctx)) { failed = true; state.errorMessage = "Only one link can open per frame."; }
  else if (!ctx.services.platform.openUrl) { failed = true; state.errorMessage = "This viewer can't open links."; }
  else {
    markOpened(ctx);
    try {
      const r = ctx.services.platform.openUrl(url);
      if (isPromiseLike(r)) { state.pending = …; ctx.requestNextFrame(); }   // result settles into state.settled
      else if (r === false) { failed = true; state.errorMessage = "The link couldn't be opened. The browser may have blocked it, or no app handles it."; }
      else { opened = true; state.errorMessage = ""; }
    } catch (e) { failed = true; state.errorMessage = "The link couldn't be opened: " + String(e); }
  }
}
if (state.pending) ctx.requestNextFrame();
ctx.output("errorMessage", state.errorMessage);
if (opened) ctx.pulse("opened");
if (failed) ctx.pulse("failed");
```

**validate(url):** trim whitespace. Empty → "Add a URL to open." The URL must start with a scheme matching `^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*:`; otherwise → "Start the URL with https:// or an app scheme like myapp://." These schemes are refused → "Links using <scheme>: can't be opened from a prototype.": `javascript`, `data`, `file`, `blob`, `vbscript`, `about`. `http`, `https`, `mailto`, `tel` and `sms` are always allowed. Any other scheme is a custom app scheme and goes to the host.

- Pulses: `open` is the only pulse input (upstream pulse or false→true edge of a connected boolean). Pulses on consecutive frames each try to open. `opened` and `failed` each last one frame, and at most one of them fires per index per frame.
- Frame 0: no side effect unless `open` pulses on frame 0; browsers usually block that because there was no user gesture, so `failed` fires.
- Loops: per-index evaluation, but at most one URL opens per patch per frame: the lowest pulsed index opens, and every other index pulsed on that frame fires `failed` with "Only one link can open per frame." A tapped item in a looped list opens only its own URL.
- Edge cases: changing `url` without a pulse does nothing. `errorMessage` keeps the latest problem until an open succeeds.
- Muted: never opens anything; `opened` and `failed` never fire; `errorMessage` outputs "".
- Async: when the platform returns a promise, the outcome is stored in `state.settled` (ignored if a newer pulse replaced it) and `opened` or `failed` fires on the first frame after it settles. Never call `ctx.output` from the callback.
- Platform and simulation: the desktop host calls Electron `shell.openExternal` for http, https, mailto, tel and sms. For other custom schemes it asks the person once per scheme per session. The web player opens a new tab with `window.open(url, "_blank", "noopener")`; it treats a `null` return as a failure, and custom schemes may show the browser's own confirmation. Browsers allow new tabs only shortly after a tap or key press, so the renderer should flush pending opens inside the pointer or key event that caused them when it can. Headless simulation never opens anything: hosts provide a stub that records the URL through `ctx.services.log("log", "Open URL:", url)` and reports success. Without a stub, the patch fails with "This viewer can't open links."
- Restart and dispose: clears `errorMessage` and pending results. Nothing to release.
- Contract gap: `PlatformServices.openUrl` returns `void`, so success can't be detected yet; treat a normal return as success and a thrown error as failure. Proposed: `openUrl?: (url: string) => boolean | Promise<boolean>`.
- Coming from Origami: Result is named `opened`. `failed` and `errorMessage` are new, and dangerous schemes are refused.

Stateless apart from `{ warned: false }` per loop index, which tracks one-time warnings.

Each frame, per loop index:
```
if (!ctx.pulsed("send")) return;
const record = lookupRecord(ctx.services, ctx.input("connection"));   // handle { kind: "webSocket", key } from WebSocket Connection
if (!record) { warnOnce("WebSocket Send needs a Connection from a WebSocket Connection patch."); return; }
if (record.phase !== "open") { record.sendError = "Couldn't send: the WebSocket isn't connected yet."; return; }
const message = ctx.input("message");
const text = ctx.typeParam === "json" ? JSON.stringify(message ?? null) : String(message ?? "");
if (record.socket.bufferedAmount > 16 * 1024 * 1024) { record.sendError = "Couldn't send: messages are going out faster than the connection can carry them."; return; }
try { record.socket.send(text); record.sendOk = true; ctx.pulse("sent"); }
catch (e) { record.sendError = "Couldn't send: " + String(e); }
```

- Pulses: `send` is the only pulse input (upstream pulse or false→true edge of a connected boolean, so a held `true` sends once). Pulses on consecutive frames each send. `sent` pulses on the frame the message was handed to the socket.
- Serialization: `text` sends the text unchanged as a text frame. `json` sends compact `JSON.stringify` output as a text frame; `NaN` and `Infinity` become `null` the way `JSON.stringify` writes them, and media values serialize as their `{ assetId }` or `{ url }` objects. Nothing is sent as binary.
- Frame 0: a pulse on frame 0 usually arrives while the connection is still connecting, so it records the "isn't connected yet" error. Send once Connected turns on instead.
- Loops: per-index evaluation. A scalar Send pulse over a looped Message sends every item as its own message, in ascending index order, on the same frame. To send the whole loop as one JSON array, convert it with Loop to Array first. A looped Send pulse sends only the pulsed indices.
- Order and errors: Send is downstream of its Connection, so it sees this frame's connection phase. Problems go into `record.sendError` and appear on the Connection patch's Error and Error Message on the next frame. A successful send sets `record.sendOk`, which lets the Connection clear an old send error.
- Edge cases: a Connection input that isn't a WebSocket handle (null, or a value from another patch) sends nothing and warns once per restart. Changing Message without a pulse sends nothing.
- Muted: never sends; `sent` never fires.
- Platform and simulation: behaves the same on every platform once a connection is open; headless hosts' socket stubs receive the text. Platform limits live on WebSocket Connection.
- Restart and dispose: resets warnings. The socket belongs to the Connection patch, so there's nothing to release here.
- Coming from Origami: Sent is new. Origami doesn't document how a looped Message is sent; Sonobe sends one message per item.

State per instance (`messages` is a `wholeLoop` output, so the patch never replicates): `{ message: <zero value of typeParam>, warned: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const record = lookupRecord(ctx.services, ctx.inputItems("connection")[0]);   // handle { kind: "webSocket", key }
if (ctx.isConnected("connection") && !record) warnOnce("WebSocket Receive needs a Connection from a WebSocket Connection patch.");
const batch = record && record.frame === ctx.frame ? record.messages : [];
const matched = [];
for (const m of batch) {
  if (ctx.typeParam === "json") {
    m.parsed ??= tryParse(m.text);            // { ok, value }, parsed once and shared by every JSON receiver
    if (m.parsed.ok) matched.push(m.parsed.value);
  } else matched.push(m.text);
}
if (matched.length > 0) state.message = matched[matched.length - 1];
ctx.output("message", state.message);
ctx.output("messages", loopOf(matched));
if (matched.length > 0) ctx.pulse("received");
ctx.requestNextFrame();                       // while connected, messages can arrive at any time
```

- Delivery: the WebSocket Connection patch moves messages that arrived since the last frame into `record.messages`, in arrival order, when it evaluates (it's upstream, so it runs first). Every Receive patch on the connection sees the same batch, so messages are broadcast and never consumed. When the Connection didn't evaluate this frame (it's muted, for example), `record.frame !== ctx.frame` and nothing is delivered.
- Type matching: `text` receives every message as its raw text, including text that happens to be JSON. `json` receives only messages that `JSON.parse` accepts, which includes bare numbers, strings and `null`; other messages are skipped silently. Binary frames arrive already decoded as UTF-8 text.
- Pulses: `received` pulses on every frame where at least one matching message arrived, even when it's identical to the previous one. It fires once per frame, however many messages arrived.
- Frame 0: `message` is the typeParam zero value (`""` for text, `null` for json), `messages` is an empty loop, and `received` doesn't fire.
- Loops: `messages` is a whole loop of every matching message from this frame, in arrival order, and it's empty on frames with none. `message` is a single value: the latest matching message, held between frames.
- Retargeting: switching the Connection input, a disconnect, or a reconnect keeps the last `message` until a matching message arrives.
- Edge cases: a Connection value that isn't a WebSocket handle delivers nothing and warns once per restart. The Connection patch caps a frame's batch at 10,000 messages.
- Muted: outputs the zero value on `message` and an empty loop on `messages`, and `received` never fires.
- Platform and simulation: identical everywhere once the connection is open. Simulation stubs push messages through the connection's platform socket, and each appears on the frame after it arrives.
- Restart and dispose: `message` resets to the zero value. Nothing to release; the socket belongs to the Connection patch.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's Message turns into a loop only on frames where several messages arrive. Sonobe keeps Message a single latest value and gives the frame's messages on Messages, so layers bound to Message don't flicker between one copy and several. Received is new; Origami needed Pulse on Change, which misses identical consecutive messages.
